---
import TimelineCalculator from '@/components/calculators/TimelineCalculator';
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Why does the projection curve flatten?',
    a: 'As you lose weight, your body needs fewer calories to run and to move, so the same intake produces a smaller deficit each week. Most calculators ignore this and promise an unrealistic straight line. Ours recalculates your maintenance every week.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will I lose weight exactly like this?',
    a: 'No. Real weight loss is noisy: water, glycogen, sodium and hormones move the scale by 1–2 kg day to day. Treat the projection as the trend line your weekly averages should roughly follow.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does the model not include?',
    a: 'Adaptive thermogenesis (the extra 5–10% drop in metabolism during long diets), changes in activity, and diet breaks. Real timelines are often 10–20% longer than projected; plan for that.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use it for gaining weight?',
    a: 'Yes. Set a goal above your current weight and an intake above maintenance. Keep surpluses small (200–300 kcal) to limit fat gain.',
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout
  slug="timeline"
  title="Weight-Loss Timeline Calculator"
  description="Find out exactly when you will reach your goal weight. A week-by-week projection that adapts as your metabolism slows, with a chart and milestone dates."
  lede="Enter your goal and daily calories to see the date you will get there, week by week. Unlike straight-line calculators, this one slows down as you do, so the date is one you can actually plan around."
  faq={faq}
>
  <TimelineCalculator client:load slot="calculator" />

  <h2>Why most timeline calculators are wrong</h2>
  <p>
    The common "3,500 calories per pound" shortcut assumes your deficit stays constant. It does not.
    A lighter body has a lower basal metabolic rate and costs less energy to move around, so a fixed
    intake produces a shrinking deficit over time. For someone losing 15 kg, that can add a month or
    more to the timeline.
  </p>
  <p>
    This tool runs a week-by-week simulation: each week it recomputes your maintenance calories from
    your projected weight using Mifflin-St Jeor, applies your intake, and converts the difference to
    weight change at 7,700 kcal per kilogram. The chart shows the resulting curve alongside the
    naive estimate so you can see the difference.
  </p>
  <h2>Making the plan hold up</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Re-run it monthly.</strong> Update your current weight and the projection tightens.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Expect noise.</strong> Compare weekly averages, not single weigh-ins. Our{' '}
      <a href="/guides/reading-the-scale/">guide to the scale</a> explains what to ignore.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Plan a diet break</strong> every 8–12 weeks for longer projects: two weeks at
      maintenance restores energy and adherence.
    </li>
  </ul>
</ToolLayout>
